5, 0.0304107666015625, -0.0166778564453125, 0.03570556640625, 0.0041351318359375, 0.015228271484375, -0.016845703125, -0.0255279541015625, 0.0127105712890625, 0.0287933349609375, 0.015838623046875, -0.0374755859375, 0.007396697998046875, -0.07464599609375, -0.01276397705078125, -0.037994384765625, -0.0039215087890625, -0.0202178955078125, -0.04815673828125, -0.0006785392761230469, 0.01091766357421875, -0.02484130859375, 0.01131439208984375, 0.03790283203125, 0.005428314208984375, -0.008056640625, -0.022003173828125, -0.029815673828125, -0.00643157958984375, -0.0003380775451660156, -0.0238800048828125, -0.0241241455078125, 0.00989532470703125, 0.038116455078125, 0.01244354248046875, -0.00829315185546875, 0.038909912109375, 0.0176544189453125, 0.0386962890625, 0.01345062255859375, -0.0089874267578125, -0.032318115234375, 0.039459228515625, 0.0193023681640625, -0.0113983154296875, -0.00888824462890625, 0.0258026123046875, 0.0247955322265625, -0.0224456787109375, 0.0158538818359375, 0.01071929931640625, -0.0291595458984375, 0.006927490234375, 0.0031833648681640625, 0.00803375244140625, -0.0231781005859375, -0.01253509521484375, 0.00353240966796875, -0.024658203125, -0.0167388916015625, 0.0167999267578125, -0.01267242431640625, 0.01462554931640625, 0.0184478759765625, 0.00711822509765625, 0.0257110595703125, -0.0399169921875, 0.016815185546875, 0.00597381591796875, 0.03399658203125, -0.0181732177734375, 0.02618408203125, 0.0212860107421875, 0.0189056396484375, -0.01157379150390625, -0.0207061767578125, 0.02838134765625, 0.006107330322265625, -0.006439208984375, 0.01580810546875, -0.001987457275390625, -0.01568603515625, 0.062225341796875, 0.00902557373046875, -0.0142059326171875, -0.0159912109375, 0.01515960693359375, 0.0216064453125, 0.019744873046875, 0.0008335113525390625, 0.0271148681640625, 0.01593017578125, -0.0033245086669921875, -0.015899658203125, 0.015625, 0.043670654296875, 0.04595947265625, -0.032562255859375, -0.0013484954833984375, 0.01062774658203125, 0.0287017822265625, -0.040374755859375, -0.00787353515625, 0.0201416015625, -0.01959228515625, 0.008392333984375, -0.0121917724609375, 0.026702880859375, 0.02984619140625, 0.033416748046875, -0.00885772705078125, 0.00362396240234375, -0.006084442138671875, 0.0220794677734375, 0.02734375, -0.0267333984375, -0.0104217529296875, -0.0357666015625, -0.048614501953125, 0.00875091552734375, -0.008880615234375, 0.0038928985595703125, -0.01824951171875, 0.0193328857421875, 0.00957489013671875, 0.00719451904296875, -0.016693115234375, -0.00986480712890625, -0.0170440673828125, 0.0139923095703125, -0.006931304931640625, 0.0201873779296875, -0.006988525390625, 0.006256103515625, -0.0174713134765625, 0.007328033447265625, -0.0089111328125, 0.0009450912475585938, 0.035980224609375, -0.01181793212890625, 0.02001953125, -0.024200439453125, -0.0177154541015625, 0.01922607421875, -0.00437164306640625, -0.00443267822265625, -0.00323486328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. In this study, \"skilled accounting judgement\" was operationalised by having three professional accountants (each with at least five years of experience and relevant certifications) annotate each invoice according to a standardised set of GL coding guidelines. These guidelines were based on the client\u2019s chart of accounts and industry best practices, specifying, for example, that IT hardware purchases should be coded as \"IT Equipment\" unless the description or vendor indicates office supplies. Ambiguity was operationally defined as cases where the invoice description, vendor, or context did not clearly map to a single GL code, such as invoices with vague descriptions like \"services rendered\" or those from vendors supplying multiple categories. Annotators flagged such cases using a dedicated annotation field. Flagged invoices were discussed in weekly consensus meetings, where each annotator presented their rationale referencing the guidelines. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion, followed by a majority vote if consensus was not reached. Approximately 10% of invoices underwent this process, and in cases where no majority emerged, a senior accountant acted as final arbiter. Inter-annotator agreement was measured on a subset of 1,000 invoices, yielding a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.89, indicating high consistency.\n\nWhile AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, there is a research gap regarding the effectiveness and interpretability of in-house small language models (SLMs) compared to larger, external models. For this study, we define \"small\" language models as transformer-based architectures with fewer than 150 million parameters, which can be fine-tuned on a single GPU and deployed on-premises. SBERT-base (~110M parameters) and DeBERTa-base (~140M parameters) were selected as representative SLMs due to their balance of efficiency and strong performance on sentence-level tasks. Although DeBERTa-base is not as small as some distilled models, it is substantially smaller than typical LLMs (e.g., GPT-3.5, >175B parameters) and is commonly used in industry for resource-constrained settings. The zero-shot GPT-3.5 baseline was included to benchmark against a widely used, general-purpose LLM, despite its known limitations for domain-specific tasks, to assess the value of in-house fine-tuning versus external APIs. The vendor identity baseline (assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor) serves as a simple heuristic reflecting common industry practice.\n\n**Background and Definitions:**  \nRecent advances in natural language processing have enabled the use of sentence transformers and SLMs for document classification. In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to an embedding space where data points are unevenly distributed, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally dense, uniform groupings within that space. \"Structured input\" denotes invoice data reformatted with explicit fields and labels, as opposed to unstructured, free-text input.\n\n**Dataset Characteristics:**  \nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. The dataset spans invoices issued between 2018 and 2023. There are 37 unique GL codes, with a long-tailed distribution: the top 5 codes account for 48% of invoices, while 12 codes have fewer than 50 examples each. Vendor frequency is similarly skewed: the top 10 vendors account for 35% of invoices, and 60% of vendors appear fewer than 10 times. Annotation was performed by three certified accountants following the documented protocol above, with periodic adjudication to ensure label quality.\n\nPreprocessing included anonymisation (removal or hashing of client and personal identifiers, but vendor names were retained as they are essential features), tokenisation, and removal of non-informative fields\u2014defined as fields with missing values in over 95% of invoices or containing only boilerplate text (e.g., page numbers, generic footers). The removal of these fields was justified to reduce noise and focus the model on relevant content; however, we acknowledge that some contextual cues may have been lost. For model evaluation, we used 5-fold cross-validation with client-level splits: invoices from each client appeared in only one fold, ensuring that test data represented unseen clients and preventing data leakage.\n\n**Model Selection and Baselines:**  \nWe evaluated two pre-trained models: SBERT-base and DeBERTa-base, as defined above. Both models were fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU for 10 epochs, using a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of 2e-5. Baselines included a zero-shot large language model (LLM; GPT-3.5, prompted without fine-tuning) and a vendor identity baseline. The choice of GPT-3.5 zero-shot reflects a practical comparison point for organisations considering external LLM APIs versus in-house SLMs, despite its lack of domain adaptation.\n\n**Geometric Analysis:**  \nTo assess embedding space geometry, we computed the isotropy coefficient (as in Ethayarajh, 2019), which measures the uniformity of embedding vector directions (values closer to 1 indicate uniformity, while values near 0 indicate concentration along certain directions). We also used silhouette scores (which quantify how similar an object is to its own cluster compared to other clusters; higher values indicate better separation) and Davies-Bouldin indices (which measure average similarity between clusters; lower values indicate more distinct clusters). Embeddings were visualised using t-SNE and UMAP to inspect cluster structure. The sentence-embedding space was globally anisotropic (isotropy coefficient < 0.3), but contained locally isotropic clusters, with clusters strongly correlated to vendor identity (average silhouette score 0.62; Davies-Bouldin index 0.88). This indicates that the models learn vendor-specific representations, which may aid classification but also risk overfitting to vendor patterns. Visualisations (not shown here due to space) confirmed tight clusters for frequent vendors and more diffuse clusters for rare vendors. The observed geometry suggests that while the models capture meaningful structure for frequent vendors, generalisation to rare vendors or new contexts may be limited.\n\n**Results:**  \nSBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieved an average accuracy of 0.96 (\u00b10.01), F1 score of 0.94 (\u00b10.02), precision of 0.95, and recall of 0.93. DeBERTa-base achieved similar but slightly lower metrics (accuracy 0.95, F1 0.92). The zero-shot LLM baseline yielded accuracy 0.85, F1 0.81, precision 0.82, and recall 0.80. The vendor identity baseline achieved accuracy 0.88, F1 0.83, precision 0.84, and recall 0.82. McNemar\u2019s test indicated that SBERT\u2019s improvement over baselines was statistically significant (p < 0.01). Per-class confusion matrices revealed that most misclassifications occurred in underrepresented GL codes (support < 50), with per-class F1 scores ranging from 0.87 (rare categories) to 0.97 (frequent categories). The most common error types were confusion between similar expense categories (e.g., \"Office Supplies\" vs. \"IT Equipment\") and misclassification of invoices with ambiguous or minimal descriptions. For rare GL codes, precision and recall dropped below 0.80 in several cases. For new clients, SBERT reached an F1 score of 0.90 (\u00b10.03) with approximately 100 client-specific invoices. Per-vendor analysis revealed consistent gains across both frequent and infrequent vendors.\n\n**Overfitting and Memorisation Analysis:**  \nTo assess overfitting to vendor identity, we conducted an ablation study by masking vendor names during both training and inference. This resulted in a 5% absolute decrease in F1 score (from 0.94 to 0.89), with the largest drops observed for vendors with high invoice counts. Additional regularisation strategies, such as dropout and label smoothing, were applied but yielded only marginal improvements (<0.01 F1). These findings suggest that while vendor information is a strong predictive feature, reliance on it may limit generalisability to new vendors or changes in vendor behaviour. For real-world deployment, we recommend periodic retraining and monitoring for vendor drift, as well as exploring domain adaptation techniques to mitigate overfitting.\n\n**Structured Input Analysis:**  \nThe structured input format, which included explicit field labels and tabular formatting, did not improve SLM performance compared to unstructured text (\u0394F1 < 0.01, not significant); error analysis suggested that the model\u2019s pre-training on natural language may limit its ability to leverage artificial structure, and that field labels sometimes introduced noise or redundancy.\n\n**Interpretability Analysis:**  \nWe applied integrated gradients and attention visualisation to a stratified random sample of 200 predictions (balanced across correct and incorrect classifications, and across frequent and rare GL codes). Integrated gradients were computed with respect to the input tokens, highlighting which words contributed most to the model\u2019s decision. In 78% of cases, vendor names and key invoice terms (e.g., \"laptop,\" \"consulting\") were primary drivers. For ambiguous invoices (e.g., \"services rendered\" with no further detail), attribution was diffuse and less informative. Attention maps were less consistent, often highlighting field labels or irrelevant tokens, especially in structured input. Quantitatively, integrated gradients provided clear rationales in 85% of correct predictions but only 40% of incorrect or ambiguous cases. These methods improved understanding of model decisions for straightforward cases but were limited for complex or borderline examples, indicating a need for more robust interpretability tools.\n\n**Error Analysis and Rare Categories:**  \nGranular"}}